# N-doped nanocarbon embedded in hierarchically porous metal-organic frameworks for highly efficient CO2 fixation

N-doped nanocarbon embedded in hierarchically porous metal-organic frameworks for highly efficient $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ fixation
1Guangdong Provincial Key Laboratory of Fuel Cell Technology, School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, South China University of Technology, Guangzhou 510640, China;   
2State Key Laboratory of Pulp and Paper Engineering, School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, South China University of Technology, Guangzhou 510640, China
The rational integration of multi-functional components with metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) to form MOF-based catalysts can often afford enhanced catalytic activity for specific reactions. Herein, we propose a novel strategy for the synthesis of hierarchically porous MOFs (e.g., MIL-101)-encapsulated N-doped nanocarbon $(\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL})$ by controlled pyrolysis of ionic liquids@MIL-101 precursors $(\mathrm{ILs}@\mathrm{MIL})$ . The obtained $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ composites not only possess abundant enlarged mesopores, but also show multi-active sites without the sacrifice of their structure stability. The $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}$ can efficiently facilitate the mass transfer of substrates, exhibiting excellent catalytic performance in the synthesis of cyclic carbonates from epoxides and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under mild and co-catalyst-free conditions (i.e., $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and ambient pressure of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ). Furthermore, the multi-active Lewis acid sites and nucleophilic sites (Br ions) as well as the strong affinity of catalysts toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ also contribute to the excellent catalytic activity of the $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ . This study might open a new avenue for the rational design of MOF-based composites by employing ILs@MOF as precursors for advanced heterogeneous catalysis.
metal-organic frameworks, N-doped nanocarbons, encapsulation, hierarchical porosity, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ fixation
Citation: Chen F, Shen K, Chen L, Li Y. N-doped nanocarbon embedded in hierarchically porous metal-organic frameworks for highly efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation. Sc China Chem, 2022, 65: 1411–1419, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-022-1298-9
## 1 Introduction
Excessive $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emission is a major cause of climate change, and the concerns on carbon emission and neutralization have spurred great interest in the development of efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ utilization technologies to reduce carbon footprint. Despite its undesirable effects, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is an inexpensive, abundant, renewable, and nontoxic $\mathrm{C}_{1}$ resource, which can be fixated into diverse value-added chemical feedstocks [1,2]. Among all the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation strategies, the cycloaddition of epoxides with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to form cyclic carbonates has been considered as one of the most promising approaches to transform $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into
diverse fine chemicals [3,4].
Currently, a variety of homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts have been developed for the coupling of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with epoxides [5–8]. However, most heterogeneous catalysts usually require a co-catalyst, which complicates the subsequent product separation. Therefore, it is highly desired to seek heterogeneous catalysts with highly active sites for cocatalyst-free $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition reactions. Recently, some studies demonstrated that carbon-based materials can be used as a new class of catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ utilization due to their unique characteristics, such as good chemical stability, low cost of manufacture, tunable surface chemistry, excellent electrical conductivity [9–11]. However, pure porous carbons often lack highly active sites and weak interactions with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, which make them relatively inert for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition even using co-catalysts. Therefore, it is still challenging to develop efficient carbon-based heterogeneous catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition under mild conditions without co-catalysts.
On the other hand, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a new class of crystalline porous materials, are constructed by the self-assembly of metal cations (or clusters) and multifunctional organic linkers [12,13]. The high porosity, exceptional tunability and adequate stability of MOFs make them extensively be applied in heterogenous catalysis, gas storage and separation [14–16]. Recently, MOFs are also considered to be good catalysts for the cycloaddition of epoxides with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ due to their tailorable functionalities and outstanding $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption ability. However, it is worth noting that most of MOFs exhibit small pore windows $(<2\mathrm{nm})$ that can undoubtedly hinder the mass transfer and limit the access of large molecules to active sites [17–19]. Besides, pristine MOFs often show undesirable catalytic performances for many complicated reactions that require multi-active sites due to their monotonous active sites [20,21]. Thus, various functionalization strategies have been developed to improve the catalytic activities of MOFs for the cycloaddition of epoxides with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , such as the fabrication of multifunctional MOFs [22,23] and the modification of MOFs with various materials including ionic liquids (ILs) and nitrogen-doped carbon [24-26].
It is well known that some ILs can be used as a new type of precursors to produce nanocarbons with unique properties by using a simple pyrolysis method [27]. Recently, Gao et al. [28] proposed a facile approach to prepare three-dimensional N-doped nanocarbon (CN) with a specific $\mathrm{sp}^{3}/\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ interface, high nitrogen content and excellent conductivity via the high-temperature pyrolysis of $\mathrm{[Hvim]NO_{3}}$ . Porous nitrogendoped carbons generated from ILs do show the structural inversion of template structures, thus enabling perfect structural control [29–31]. Herein, we introduce a facile onestep pyrolysis strategy with which can in-situ encapsulate N-doped nanocarbons in MOFs and achieve multi-functional catalytic sites in the obtained MOF-based composites. We show that the 1-butyl-3-methylimidazolium bromide (BmimBr) in the pores of MIL-101 can be selectively cleaved in a well-controlled pyrolysis process, which not only allows for the generation of tunable porous structures, but also can produce N-doped nanocarbon highly dispersed in the MOF framework. Benefiting from the synergistic effects between the abundant Br ions adsorbed in N-doped nanocarbon and the unsaturated Cr sites as well as the unique hierarchically porous structure, the resulting composites, namely $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ , can serve as efficient catalysts for the synthesis of cyclic carbonates from epoxides and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under mild and co-catalyst-free condition.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Preparation of $\mathbf{CN}@\mathbf{ML}$ composites
As one of the most studied MOFs, MIL-101 is employed as an example to illustrate our strategy because of its excellent stability toward ILs, and potential advantages for catalytic applications [32,33]. First, BmimBr was encapsulated in MIL-101 by a wet-impregnation method (Figure 1), affording the BmimBr $@$ MIL(0.67) composite with $67\mathrm{wt\%}$ loading of BmimBr. The sharp decrease of pore volume (from $1.{\overset{-}{2}}7\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{3}\ {\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ to $0\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and specific surface area (from $2{,}605\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to $0.65\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ measured by $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/ desorption experiments implies that most of pores in BmimBr@MIL(0.67) have been occupied by BmimBr molecules (Table S1, Figures S1 and S2). The FTIR spectrum of BmimBr@MIL exhibits typical characteristics of both MIL101 and BmimBr (Figure S3), indicating that the structure of MIL-101 is well maintained after loading BmimBr. In addition, as shown in Figure S4, the XRD characteristic peaks of MIL-101 can be obviously observed for BmimBr@MIL (0.67), which indicates that the MIL-101 framework is well maintained after loading BmimBr. However, the disappearance of some low-angle diffraction peaks in the XRD pattern of BmimBr@MIL-101(0.67) indicates that most of its ordered mesopores have been occupied by the BmimBr (Figure S4) [34]. Additionally, scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images of BmimBr@MIL(0.67) (Figure S5c and S5d) show wellshaped octahedral morphology that is similar to parent MIL101 (Figure S5a and S5b). The elemental mapping images clearly show that N, Br, and Cr are uniformly distributed throughout BmimBr@MIL(0.67), indicating that BmimBr molecules are located inside the pores of MIL-101 instead of being dispersed on its surface (Figures S5e–S5h).
To control the porosity of the as-obtained materials, we prepared a series of BmimBr@MIL with BmimBr loadings $(w)$ from 0.5 to 0.71, which are denoted as BmimBr ${\cdot}_{\mathcal{O}\mathrm{ML}}$ $(w)$ , respectively. It is worth noting that BmimBr would not be lossy during the preparation process, and thus we can precisely control the loading amount of BmimBr in MIL-101 by adding different amount of BmimBr in the synthesis. All the $\mathrm{BmimBr}@\mathrm{MIL}(\boldsymbol{w})$ samples were further pyrolyzed in an argon atmosphere to achieve a dense-to-porous structure transformation. The resultant materials are denoted as $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(T,\ w;$ time), where $^{\ast}T^{\prime}$ and “time” represent pyrolysis temperature and pyrolysis time, respectively.
First, the thermal stability of BmimBr, MIL-101 and $\mathrm{BmimBr}@\mathrm{MIL}(\boldsymbol{w})$ was investigated by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) (Figure S6 and Table S2). As compared with MIL-101 that is collapsed at ca. $339^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the pyrolysis process of $\mathrm{BmimBr}@\mathrm{MIL}(\boldsymbol{w})$ can be divided into two steps: i.e., the disintegration of the incorporated BmimBr at around $240^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the collapse of MIL-101 at around $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Obviously, the MIL-101 frameworks in $\mathrm{BmimBr}@\mathrm{MIL}(\boldsymbol{w})$ composites show improved thermal stability as compared with pristine MIL-101, probably due to the strong interaction between BmimBr and MIL-101 and the space-occupying effect of BmimBr in the nanocages of MIL-101 to prevent the porous structure from collapsing [24,35,36]. In addition, we also performed the DSC thermograms of pure BmimBr and confined BmimBr in BmimBr $@$ MIL(0.67). As shown in Figure S7, the glass transition temperature $(T_{\mathrm{g}})$ of the pristine BmimBr is $-61.5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , while that of the confined BmimBr increases to $-57.7^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . These results further confirm that most of the BmimBr molecules in BmimBr@MIL(0.67) have been successfully encapsulated into the pores of MIL101 [37]. Accordingly, we choose $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as the pyrolysis temperature to investigate the effect of BmimBr loading and pyrolysis time on the pore structure of the resultant $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ .
Subsequently, the $\mathrm{BmimBr}@\mathrm{MIL}(\boldsymbol{w})$ composites were pyrolyzed at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ to obtain the corresponding $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,\boldsymbol{w},30)$ (Figure S8). As expected, the porous structure of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,\:w,\:30)$ could be well tuned by carefully controlling the loading amount of BmimBr. As shown in Figures $2\mathrm{a}{-}2\mathrm{e}$ and ${\mathsf{S9a}}{\mathsf{-S9e}}$ , all the obtained $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,\:w,\:30)$ materials show a sponge-like structure with controllable mesopores, among which $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ (400, 0.67, 30) has the most abundant mesopore structure since electron beams can easily penetrate through its octahedron framework with the weakest contrast. For comparison, MIL-101 was also pyrolyzed under $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . No obvious mesopores can be observed in the resultant MIL (400, 30) (Figure 2a and S9a), which confirms that the encapsulated BmimBr molecules play a key role in the denseto-porous transformation for BmimBr@MIL(w) to
$\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,w,30)$ .
The $\Nu_{2}$ isotherms of MIL(400, 30) show a sole type I curve with very low total ${\bf N}_{2}$ uptakes, indicating the collapse of MIL-101 frameworks and no visible porosity in the structure (Figures 2i and 2j). However, all of the $\Nu_{2}$ isotherms of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,\boldsymbol{w},30)$ can be identified as a combination of both type-I and type-IV curves with obvious hysteresis loops, which reveal their micro-/meso-porous structures (Figures 2i and 2j). When the loading amounts of BmimBr increase from 0.5 to 0.67, the BET surface areas and pore volumes are increased from $694\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.84~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to $1,348\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1.50\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Table S1). However, by further increasing the loading amounts of BmimBr to 0.71, the BET surface area and pore volume of the resulting $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.71,30$ ) decrease to $(793\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1.05\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , because the excessive BmimBr-derived nanocarbon can cover the surface of MIL101 and block its pores. Correspondingly, the pore-size distribution curves in Figure 2j indicate that all of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ $(400,\ w,\ 30)$ contain two kinds of pores: (1) micro-/mesopores (smaller than $3.5\mathrm{nm}$ ) resulting from intrinsic MIL-101 frameworks; (2) large mesopores (from about 4 to $20\mathrm{nm}$ ) newly generated in the controlled pyrolysis process.
The structural stability of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,\boldsymbol{w},30)$ and MIL (400, 30) was investigated by XRD. As shown in Figure S10, the MIL-101 structure of MIL(400, 30) is obviously collapsed after the pyrolysis treatment. However, when BmimBr is added and thus its loading is increased to 0.67, the XRD characteristic peaks of MIL-101 for $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ $(400,w,30)$ start to appear and become increasingly stronger, since BmimBr-derived nanocarbon can prevent the MIL-101 frameworks from collapsing. To our delight, there is no significant crystallinity loss for CN@MIL(400, 0.67, 30) as proven by their strong characteristic XRD peaks. However, further increasing the BmimBr loading to 0.71, the crystallinity of the resultant $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}(400,0.71,30)$ rapidly decreases. Therefore, 0.67 is the most favorable loading amount to obtain the desirable $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,\:w,\:30)$ with high porosity and crystallinity, which is well corresponding to the BET results. Moreover, we have investigated the chemical stability of the $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,\ 0.67,\ 30)$ in the aqueous solutions at different pH. Although abundant large mesopores have been formed, the obtained CN@MIL(400, 0.67, 30) still has the similar chemical stability to the pristine MIL-101 at the $\mathsf{p H}$ of 1, 2 and 12 (Figure S11). The porosity of the $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67$ , time) material can also be well controlled by varying the pyrolysis time. As shown in Figures 2f–2h, when $\mathrm{BmimBr@MIL}(0.67)$ is pyrolyzed at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 15 to $120\mathrm{min}$ , the mesopore structures of the resultant samples become more and more obvious (Figures $2\mathrm{f}{-}2\mathrm{h}$ and S9f–S9h), and the $\Nu_{2}$ uptakes of micropores in the $P/P_{0}$ range of 0–0.5 rapidly decrease with the pyrolysis time (Figure $2\mathrm{k}$ and 2l). However, the XRD patterns in Figure S12 reveal that the MIL-101 framework is almost collapsed when the thermolysis time is over $60\mathrm{min}$ . In addition, the TG results (Figure S13 and Table S3) reveal that although abundant larger mesopores have been formatted inside the framework, $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,\ 0.67,\ 30)$ still shows similar thermal stability as the parent MIL-101.
## 2.2 Mechanistic study of the formation of larger mesopores
In addition, a temperature-dependent experiment was also carried out to acquire more information in the pyrolysis process of $\mathrm{BmimBr@MIL}(0.67)$ . The XRD patterns in Figure S14 demonstrate that the crystallinity of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(T,$ 0.67, 30) can be well maintained when the pyrolysis temperature is below $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . However, if the temperature increases to $425^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the framework of MIL-101 would be completely destroyed. The corresponding change in color from brown to dark is also observed with an increase in the pyrolysis temperature (Figure S8). Interestingly, the BET surface areas of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(350,0.67,30)$ and $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ (375, 0.67, 30) are calculated to be 2,393 and $2{,}233\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Table S1), respectively, which are only slightly lower than that of parent MIL-101 $(2,605\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . Unfortunately, when the temperature is below $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , no obvious mesopores are produced in the obtained materials (Figures S15–S17, Table S1). Based on the above results, abundant mesopores from about 4 to $20\mathrm{nm}$ observed in $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ can be reasonably ascribed to the partial decarboxylation of ligands in the pyrolysis process of BmimBr@MIL(0.67), where the space-occupying effect of BmimBr-derived Ndoped carbon in the nanocages of MIL-101 can efficiently retain the framework structure of parent MIL-101.
## 2.3 Characterizations of CN@MIL(400, 0.67, 30)
For comparison, BmimBr was also pyrolyzed at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain $\mathrm{BmimBr}(400,30)$ . As expected, a broad XRD band centered at about $25^{\circ}$ for $\mathrm{BmimBr}(400,30)$ is in agreement with the (002) planes of graphite (Figure S18), which is further confirmed by the selected area electron diffraction image (inset of Figure S19b) [38,39]. In addition, STEMEDX element mapping images reveal that $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ and Br atoms have been successfully anchored onto the carbon matrix of BmimBr(400, 30) (Figure S19).
The surface morphology and internal structure of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ were investigated by SEM, TEM and HAADF-STEM. As shown in Figure 4a and Figure S20, there are numerous mesopores on the surface of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ (400, 0.67, 30), which cannot be observed on those of the parent MIL-101 and MIL(400,30) (Figures S5a and S9a). Meanwhile, the SEM image (Figure 4b) of a selected broken particle clearly reveals the inner porous structure of CN@MIL(400, 0.67, 30), as also confirmed by the TEM image in Figure 4c. For comparison, the inner structures of parent MIL-101 and MIL(400, 30) are compact, which are much different from that of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ , further confirming the highly porous structure of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,$ 0.67, 30) (Figure S21). The corresponding STEM-EDX elemental mapping images (Figures 4d–4h) indicate that all the Cr, N, Br elements are homogeneously dispersed in $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,\ 0.67,\ 30)$ . For comparison, we also investigated the porosity and crystallinity of $\mathrm{MIL}(T,30)$ by $\Nu_{2}$ sorption and XRD measurements (Figures S22–S24, Table S1). As shown in Figures S23 and S24, all of the $\mathrm{MIL}(T,30)$ materials show a typical type-I curve with significantly decreased $\Nu_{2}$ uptakes in the low $P/P_{0}$ range as compared with parent MIL-101, indicating the collapse and/or shrinkage of the MIL-101 framework, along with the formation of microporous carbon-based structures without detectable hierarchical pores.
## 2.4 Evaluation of catalytic performances
To simultaneously investigate the advantages of hierarchical pores and multi-active sites of CN@MIL(400, 0.67, 30), we evaluated the catalytic performances of the as-synthesized materials in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition with large-size epoxides in a Schlenk tube (Figure S26). To our delight, $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ (400, 0.67, 30) exhibits an excellent catalytic activity and achieves $96\%$ yield towards the cycloaddition of 4-(2,3- epoxypropoxy)carbazole (4-EPC) to 4-(((9H-carbazol-4-yl) oxy)methyl)-1,3-dioxolan-2-one (4-CDO) at 1 bar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the absence of co-catalysts (Table S4). The structure of 4-CDO was verified by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) and $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR (see the supporting information) [44]. Actually, to the best of our knowledge, $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ can also rank as one of the best heterogeneous catalysts for this reaction under atmospheric pressure and co-catalyst-free conditions (Table S5). By contrast, both of MIL(400,30) and BmimBr(400, 30) show much lower catalytic activities than $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ under the same condition, giving only $0\%$ and $8\%$ yields of 4-CDO after $18\mathrm{h}$ of reaction, respectively (Figure 5a, Table S4). For comparison, the physical mixture of MIL-101 and BmimBr can only afford $20\%$ yield of the target product due to its limited active sites and small pore windows (Table S4).
Subsequently, with 4-EPC as a model substrate, the effect of solvents on the catalytic activity is investigated. Furthermore, the alkaline solvents of $N_{\mathbf{\ell}}$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone and DMF are found to be more advantageous to afford 4-CDO than other solvents, and DMF is proven to be the most appropriate solvent (Table S6), which is probably due to its good $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ solubility that can efficiently promote the reaction kinetics [45]. Moreover, the influence of reaction temperature on the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ is also evaluated. As shown in Figure 5b, the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ is obviously boosted when the reaction temperature increases from $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . However, further increasing the temperature to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ results in a slight decrease of the catalytic activity because the concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the liquid phase would be gradually decreased with an increase in the reaction temperature. In addition, the reaction rate is relatively slow in the initial stage of reactions (0–2 h). This phenomenon can likely be attributed to the fact that the concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the solution is relatively low in the initial stage of reactions (Figures 5a–c).
Furthermore, the influence of catalyst dosage on the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,\ 0.67,\ 30)$ was also investigated (Figure 5c). When the dosage increases from 10 to $30~\mathrm{mg}$ , the yield of 4-CDO also increases from $78\%$ to $96\%$ after $18\mathrm{h}$ . However, further increasing the catalyst dosage to $40\mathrm{mg}$ , the yield of 4-CDO is slightly decreased. To verify the advantage of mesopores in boosting the mass transfer and thus catalytic activity, various CN@MIL( $T,w;$ , time) samples were used as catalysts for the cycloaddition reaction of 4- EPC with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . As shown in Figure 5d–f, CN@MIL(400, 0.67, 30) still shows the highest yield of 4-CDO among all the studied catalysts under the same reaction conditions. This can be attributed to its most abundant mesopores and highly exposed Lewis acidic sites, which can facilitate the diffusion of substrates and enhance the catalytic efficiency of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}(400,0.67,30)$ .
After the removal of CN@MIL(400, 0.67, 30) from the reaction mixture, no further reaction of 4-EPC with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can be observed (Figure S27), revealing the heterogeneous feature of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ in this reaction. The concentration of $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ in reaction solution was measured by Mohr titration which shows that the leaked $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ is less than $0.02\mathrm{wt.}\%$ , indicating no significant leaching of $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ in the reaction process. Atomic absorption spectroscopy (AAS) analysis further reveals that the $\mathrm{Cr}$ content in the reaction solution is below the detection limit, implying no significant $\mathrm{Cr}$ leaching of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ occurring during the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation process. Then, the stability and reusability of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ were investigated. As shown in Figure 6d, $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ can be recovered and reused for at least six runs without an obvious deactivation. XRD patterns in Figure S28 show no remarkable crystallinity loss of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ after the reaction, further confirming its good stability.
Encouraged by the outstanding catalytic performance of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition with 4- EPC, we further studied the scope of this catalyst for the reactions of various epoxides with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Table S7). Excellent yields were obtained for all the investigated epoxides, including terminal epoxides with both electron-withdrawing and electron-donating substituents, which suggests the good substrate tolerance of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ for such a type of reactions.
As the Lewis acid and Lewis base sites (i.e., ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ in MIL101 and $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ in N-doped carbon) are the two important types of active sites in $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ , pyridine-adsorption Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (Py-FTIR) was first used to determine the Lewis acidity of ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ sites. As clearly shown in
Figure 6a, the spectrum of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ exhibits a set of bands at 1,013, 1,045 and $1{,}070\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which can be assigned to the $\nu_{1}$ , $\nu_{12}$ and $\nu_{\mathrm{18a}}$ ring modes of adsorbed pyridine, respectively. The remarkably positive shifts of the $\nu_{1}$ and $\nu_{12}$ ring modes are in accordance with the observed results in the liquid phase $(+25\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $+15\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , indicating the formation of coordinated pyridine species [46]. These results reveal that strong Lewis acid sites exist in the $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}(400,0.67,30)$ .
Considering that the enhanced affinity toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules may be beneficial to the fixation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with epoxide, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption isotherm of $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ was measured and compared with that of MIL(400, 30) (Figure S29). Undoubtedly, $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}(400,0.67,30)$ shows a much higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake (1.29 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 1 bar) than MIL(400, 30) $\mathrm{0.96\mmol{g}^{-1}}$ at 1 bar) due to its higher surface area and more accessible unsaturated ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ sites. To further confirm that the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Cr}$ sites can boost the activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,\ 0.67,\ 30)$ before and after $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption was further investigated by FTIR. As shown in Figure 6b, after exposure to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , a new characteristic peak (corresponding to the stretching frequency of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}_{,}$ ) can be observed at $2{,}354~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which is blue-shifted as compared with that of free $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(2,349\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ [47]. This can be attributed to the $\nu_{3}$ stretching mode of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ forming $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}{\cdot}{\cdot}{\cdot}0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C-}0$ adducts with a linear and end-on configuration [48]. Another experiment was also carried out under the same conditions on MIL(400, 30). No obvious change can be observed in the FTIR spectrum of MIL(400, 30) after $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption.
Therefore, it can be concluded that both of the Lewis acid sites $(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+})$ and Lewis base sites $\mathrm{(Br^{-})}$ in the $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,$ 0.67, 30) contribute to the efficient cycloaddition of epoxides with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the absence of co-catalysts.
Based on above experimental results, we proposed a plausible reaction mechanism for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition of epoxides to form cyclic carbonates over $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,$ 0.67, 30) (Figure 6c). First, the epoxide is adsorbed and polarized by the coordinatively unsaturated ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ sites (Lewis acid sites) in $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,\ 0.67,\ 30)$ [43,49]. Subsequently, the ring-opening step occurs due to the nucleophilic attack of the $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ from $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ on the less sterically hindered carbon atom of the epoxide. Then, the C atom of an adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule is attacked by the ringopened intermediate. Afterward, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ inserts into the ringopened intermediate to give an acyclic ester. Finally, the intramolecular cyclization of the acyclic ester results in the formation of cyclic carbonates, followed by the regeneration of the $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ catalyst.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, we have developed a novel strategy to construct $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ by a thermal transformation method using BmimBr@MIL as precursors. The mesopore sizes can be regulated in the range of 4 to $20\mathrm{nm}$ , and highly active sites are generated by carefully controlling the thermolysis conditions and loading amounts of BmimBr. The encapsulated
BmimBr inside the MIL-101 framework plays a vital role in enlarging the pore size and maintaining the porosity of the resultant materials. It has been demonstrated that the unsaturated $\mathrm{Cr}$ sites and Br doping in $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{ML}$ significantly improve their catalytic activities in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation reactions. As exemplified by the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition with 4-EPC, the optimized $\mathrm{CN}@\mathrm{MIL}(400,0.67,30)$ material exhibits an extremely high activity with the $96\%$ yield of 4-CDO under the co-catalyst-free condition, which is much more active than the MIL-101, MIL(400,30) and BmimBr(400, 30) counterparts due to the improved mass transfer and abundant multi-functional sites. This controlled pyrolysis strategy can not only provide new insight into the pyrolyzation process of ILs encapsulated inside MOF frameworks, but also offer an alternative and efficient way to integrate MOFs with many other functional large molecules for advanced applications.
